.THS Inews 8L News.SH NAMEInews - NetNews Management System.SH SYNOPSIS.raw 0inews [-v] [-a] [-g=<grplist>] [-d=<distlist>] [-t] [-D]      [-e=<days>] [-E=<days>] [-r]      [-h] [-R]      [-c=<ctrlmsg>:<ctrl-opts>]      [-M=<msgid>].noraw.SH DESCRIPTION.ti +3Inews is the program which does the whole management on the local newssystem. In normal use it is started without any option and parses theheader, then write the article in the right directory matching the givennewsgroups. A special kind of a message is the control message (refer toRFC1036) where only a few are executed immediately, other are mailed tothe news administrator. To execute these you have to start inews withthe option.B -ato switch to admin mode. An explaination of the options follows..in +3.O -aRuns inews in admin mode to execute all fed control messages. Thisoption is obsolete, to enter admin mode you have to be user news orsuperuser..NO -e=<days>Starts to delete all article which have been arrived here more than<days> ago. If a list of groups is given, only these groups areaffected..NO -E=<days>This option is used in combination with `-e'.  While `-e' give the timeto expire old articles, `-E' gives the time to delete the Message-ID: fromthe history database.  It is useful to keep a historyfile as large aspossible to avoid duplicate article. .NO -rRebuild the active file, mostly used in combination with `-e' torecollect the articles in each group. If no groups are specified allgroups are updated..NO -hInews adds all missing headerlines in the article and executes inewsrecrusively. This option may be used when writing a new article and feedit incompletely to inews..NO -vAll logmessages are then also written to stderr..NO -g=<groups>This is a list of groups (seperated by commas) where the actual actionmay take place. Mostly used in combination with `-h', `-e' and `-r'. Inuse with `-e' the list may also contain wildcards as known by the shell(these are.B ?and.BR * )..NO -d=<dist>This is a list of distributions (seperated by commas) to where themessage should be send. Mostly used with `-h' and `-c' (see below)..NO -c=<ctrlmsg>This creates a control message which may change something on the local(and on remote) news systems. The syntax is.ce-c=<ctrlmsg>[:<option>]{ <option>}All in RFC1036 mentioned control messages are availble, only the `ihave'and `sendme' aren't supported. Refer to RFC1036 for details on eachcontrol message..NO -M=<msgid>If an external process wants to retreive an article with a given messageid and doesn't know which filename belongs to this id he can call inewswith this option and the required message id. If he wants to retreivemore than one, this option can be given as often as desired. The outputwill be either.ce 3+<message-id> <TAB> <filename>or-<message-id>where the first line will be written, if the message id was found,otherwise the other line will be the format of the output..EO.in -3.SH CAVEATS.ti +3Inews isn't a complete newssystem, but just a working subset. It istested against BNews 2.11 and seems to work okay. It won't work witholder ANews versions..SH BUGS.ti +3Maybe a lot. But none known. It runs just fine for years on severalsystems until now..SH FILES.O /h0/SPOOL/NEWSHere in all articles are placed in subdirectories..NO /h0/USR/LIB/NEWSAll configuration files are placed here, these are:.O ActiveThe active list of newsgroups..NO AliasesA list of aliases for newsgroups..NO ArchiveIf articles of a specific group shouldn't be deleted but archived duringan expire this files contains details which groups are affected andwhere to store them..NO CheckgroupsA kist to limit checkgroups depending on the distribution..NO ConvtableIf you forward news to a system which only supports 7 bit character andyou use locally 8 bit character this files descripes how to map thesecharacters. This file can also map 7 bit to 7 bit (if you want to changesomething, e.g. replace every tab by two spaces.).NO DistributionsThe default distributions for several newsgroups..NO FollowupThis describes default followups to some newsgroups, e.g. a followup inalt.sources should be placed in alt.sources.d..NO LogfileThe logfile of news; if not existing, it isn't written..NO SysThe systems to feed news to..NO send_uunameThe file to be send on an senduuname control message..EO.NO /h0/USR/LIB/NEWS/HISTORY or.NO /h0/USR/LIB/NEWS/HistoryDBM.(dir|pag)All arrived article IDs to prevent duplicate articles (the 2nd format isvalid, if you are using ndbm.).EO.SH SYSINFO.raw 4MNEWS.BATCH,s=/h0/SPOOL/BATCHMNEWS.DIR,s=/h0/SPOOL/NEWSMNEWS.LIB,s=/h0/USR/LIB/NEWSlck.inews,lhostname,s=<your hostname>hostdomain,s=<your hostdomain>.in +3Refer to source for details..in -3.noraw.SH ENVIROMENT.O USERis used if no other way can be found to get the login nameof an user..NO NAME.NO FULLNAMEis used if no other way can be found to get the fullnameof an user..NO ORGANIZATIONis used if no other way can be found to get the organizationof an user..EO.SH SEE ALSO rnews - in this distribution sbatch - in this distribution RFC1036 - found by your newsfeed (hope so)